edb/ref 1071 "Evaluate ${\\int }_{1}^{e}{ln}^{2}⁡({x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186250314 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "25b1fd19-3728-5b2c-98ff-e3d6d85297ff" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186250314 #edb/ref 1074 "We need to use integration by parts. We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={ln}^{2}⁡({x}^{3})\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=1\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\,\\text{d}x=x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {ln}^{2}⁡({x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({ln}^{2}⁡({x}^{3}))(x)-\\int (x)(\\frac{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x{ln}^{2}⁡({x}^{3})-6\\int \\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate $\\int \\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})\\text{d}x.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{3}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=1\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\,\\text{d}x=x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3}))(x)-\\int (x)(\\frac{3}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})-3\\int \\text{d}x \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})-3x+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a constant. \n\nSo, we get that the indefinite integral is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {ln}^{2}⁡({x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x & =x{ln}^{2}⁡({x}^{3})-6(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})-3x+D) \\\\  & =x{ln}^{2}⁡({x}^{3})-6x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})+18x+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-6D.$ \n\nFinally then, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}{ln}^{2}⁡({x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x & ={[x{ln}^{2}⁡({x}^{3})-6x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})+18x]}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =[e{ln}^{2}⁡({e}^{3})-6e\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{3})+18e]-[(1){ln}^{2}⁡({1}^{3})-6(1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡({1}^{3})+18(1)] \\\\  & =9e-18e+18e-18 \\\\  & =9e-18.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319432 #edb/ref 1080 "-6\\pi " #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319432 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319432 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0f8475c2-b6a4-51c9-afc7-33b04db0b7df" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186320004 #edb/ref 1080 "{\\pi }^{2}-4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186320004 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186320004 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c8d98ca1-5383-519e-939b-8606ea79f890" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186320377 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186319432 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186320377 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186546388 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186320377 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186320377 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186320377 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186546388 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186320377 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "6714774d-7da9-5f4e-89a7-0e0c1ff30a6f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186320624 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186320004 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186320624 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186546634 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186320624 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186320624 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186320624 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186546634 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186320624 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ded71c5d-94de-520f-9363-1d054bf11cec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186320942 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320624 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186320942 #edb/ref 1069 "q-352028" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186320942 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186320942 #edb/ref 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}27{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186320942 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ce8ce801-4acf-5ae8-b6e4-25107fda723b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186320942 #edb/ref 1074 "We need to use integration by parts. We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=27{x}^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=54x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 27{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(27{x}^{2})(-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3})-\\int (-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3})(54x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-9{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+18\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we use integration by parts to evaluate $\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x & \\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3})-\\int (\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3})\\cdot 1\\,dx \\\\  & =\\frac{x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{9}+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is an integral constant. \n\nSo, we get that the indefinite integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 27{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-9{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+18\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-9{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+18(\\frac{x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{9}+D) \\\\  & =-9{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+6x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=18D.$\n\nFinally, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}27{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={[-9{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+6x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)]}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =9{(\\frac{\\pi }{3})}^{2}-2-2 \\\\  & ={\\pi }^{2}-4 \\\\  & ={\\pi }^{2}-4\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186320955 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320377 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186320955 #edb/ref 1069 "q-352118" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186320955 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186320955 #edb/ref 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186320955 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "15526c87-56a5-504c-8ad1-55f1320c8be7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186320955 #edb/ref 1074 "We need to use integration by parts. We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=3{x}^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=6x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(3{x}^{2})(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x))-\\int (\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x))(6x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)-6\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we use integration by parts to evaluate $\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x & \\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x))-\\int (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x))\\cdot 1\\,dx \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,dx \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is an integral constant. \n\nSo, we get that the indefinite integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)-6\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)-6(-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+D) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+6x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-6D.$\n\nFinally, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={[3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+6x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)]}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =(3(\\pi )^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )+6(\\pi )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi ))-(3(0)^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+6(0)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =0+6\\pi (-1)-0-0 \\\\  & =-6\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186429336 #edb/ref 1030 1.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186429336 #edb/ref 1032 "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account foundations: 1 rises / 1 eligible passed source activities = 1.000000; nearest selected fraction = 1. Crossing activity numbers [1]; waits [1]. Separate account estimates: foundations=1 (1/1). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: False. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing coverage; a multi-band rise counts once. This does not interpret color steps as physical repetition units. Method: encompassing-report.md. Evidence: reference/mathacademy/encompassing-analysis-2026-10-11/frequency-fractions/foundations-frequency-estimates.json, pair 416->1116. All account evidence retained in the prepared import's selected-relationships.json." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186429336 #edb/ref 1036 #edb/ref 17592186050690 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186429337 #edb/ref 1030 0.3333333333333333 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186429337 #edb/ref 1032 "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account foundations: 1 rises / 3 eligible passed source activities = 0.333333; nearest selected fraction = 1/3. Crossing activity numbers [2]; waits [2]. Separate account estimates: foundations=1/3 (1/3). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name;