#edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546941 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546941 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186546383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186546941 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e456e79c-9696-5fd5-914c-4b58ab494688" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547030 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186546941 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547030 #edb/ref 1069 "q-346501" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547030 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547030 #edb/ref 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{e}18{x}^{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547030 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "88eae5cb-fb52-512e-a423-9c404ac93779" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547030 #edb/ref 1074 "We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2} & \\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=18{x}^{2} & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int 18{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=6{x}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 18{x}^{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}(6{x}^{3})-\\int (6{x}^{3})(\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6{x}^{3}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}-12\\int {x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate $\\int {x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & \\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={x}^{2} & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3})-\\int (\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3})(\\frac{1}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{9}+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is an integral constant. \n\nSo, we get that the indefinite integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 18{x}^{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =6{x}^{3}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}-12\\int {x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6{x}^{3}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}-12(\\frac{{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{9}+D) \\\\  & =6{x}^{3}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}-4{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{3}-12D \\\\  & ={x}^{3}(6{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\frac{4}{3})+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-12D.$\n\nFinally, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}18{x}^{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2}\\text{d}x & ={[{x}^{3}(6{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\frac{4}{3})]}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =[{e}^{3}(6{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡e)}^{2}-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡e+\\frac{4}{3})]-[{1}^{3}(6{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡1)}^{2}-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+\\frac{4}{3})] \\\\  & =[{e}^{3}(6-4+\\frac{4}{3})]-\\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{10{e}^{3}-4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547049 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186546867 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547049 #edb/ref 1069 "q-114875" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547049 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547049 #edb/ref 1071 "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547049 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bc404082-c599-5306-899d-26fb5a707805" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547049 #edb/ref 1074 "We need to use integration by parts. We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=3{x}^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=6x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(3{x}^{2})(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2})-\\int (\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2})(6x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}-3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we use integration by parts to evaluate $\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x & \\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\text{d}x=-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x(-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2})-\\int (-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2})\\cdot 1\\,dx \\\\  & =-\\frac{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,dx \\\\  & =-\\frac{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{4}+D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is an integral constant. \n\nSo, we get that the indefinite integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}-3\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}-3(-\\frac{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{4}+D) \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}+\\frac{3x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}-\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{4}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-3D.$ Finally then, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={[\\frac{3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}+\\frac{3x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}-\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{4}]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )}{2}+\\frac{3(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )}{2}-\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )}{4}-(\\frac{3(0)^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)}{2}+\\frac{3(0)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)}{2}-\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)}{4}) \\\\  & =0+\\frac{3\\pi (-1)}{4}-0-0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547115 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "64a57aef-d6e9-5810-96e6-0a4d5d4de0bb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547115 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186320955 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547135 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186547150 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547135 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a4bbd805-d804-517d-a8e5-32021f33212a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547135 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186547030 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547138 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186547135 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547138 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "aeb6cf6a-16c5-5b68-852d-5618fe48037e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547138 #edb/ref 